+# Native candidate: physical validation sheet
+
+No rows below are certified by compilation or the automated checks. Use the actual Windows and Android apps, not the legacy browser PoC. Record OS/build, app hashes, device model, storage space, router/hotspot owner, band/channel, negotiated link rate, wall-clock elapsed time, byte count and SHA-256 for each run.
+
+| Configuration / test | Status | Evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Windows startup, installation, DPAPI identity, Schannel TLS, restart | Partial | User screenshot confirms the app reaches its listening state. Exact device/OS details, native handshake, installer/upgrade and restart still require validation. |
+| CMF Phone 1: install, pairing approval/rejection, folders, both directions | Not run | Physical device required. |
+| Samsung Tab S9: install, pairing, layout, folders, both directions | Not run | Physical device required. |
+| Windows → phone + tablet, sequential and per-file round-robin | Not run | Check only one outgoing recipient active; pause one and verify next proceeds. |
+| Same LAN with internet disabled | Not run | Explicit Android Wi-Fi routing implemented; validate device behavior. |
+| Existing Windows hotspot → both Android devices | Not run | No automatic hotspot management. |
+| Existing phone/tablet hotspot → Windows | Not run | Manufacturer routing varies. |
+| Wi-Fi disconnect/reconnect during each direction | Not run | Check durable offset, final bytes and no overwrite. |
+| Android screen locked, battery saver, background, 15 idle minutes | Not run | Record Android/OEM foreground-service restrictions. |
+| Windows app restart; Android process kill/restart; phone reboot | Not run | Reopen apps and resume while session remains valid. |
+| Zero-byte, pictures, video, audio, documents, archives, Unicode/reserved names | Not run | Verify correct destination, gallery visibility and collisions. |
+| Actual 5 GB, 10 GB, and 60,000,000,000-byte file, both directions | Not run | Boundary metadata tests are insufficient evidence. |
+| Disk full, revoked source permission, deleted/changed source | Not run | Must report an actionable state and preserve completed files. |
+| Session revocation mid-chunk, expiration, foreign-session requests, wrong pin | Partial automated | Native TLS and actual Android Keystore/OS still need physical verification. |
+| Certified ≥1,000,000,000 payload bits/sec | Not run | Time from first payload request through verified receiver completion, record hashing preparation separately. No Wi-Fi link-rate substitution. |
+| Owner-signed installers/APK, upgrade and uninstall | Not run | Current Windows binary is unsigned; APK uses a development key. |
+
+For each direction, compare hashes from independently read source and completed destination. Attempt interrupted transfer recovery at the first chunk, middle, last chunk, after local flush but before acknowledgement, and during verification/publication. Keep completed received files while clearing history and cleaning cancelled partials.
+
+Release approval requires the outstanding scope/security/reliability gates to be explicitly resolved and the applicable rows to have reproducible evidence. Do not publish this candidate as a certified 1 Gbps or 60 GB-tested product.
diff --git a/docs/GETTING-STARTED.md b/docs/GETTING-STARTED.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..4032a2e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/GETTING-STARTED.md
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# Try the native v1 candidate
+
+This development build targets Windows 11 x64 and Android 10+. It is for device validation. Windows is unsigned and Android uses a debug signing key; these are not the owner-signed public release packages.
+
+1. Extract the Windows ZIP into a normal folder. Run `Install-Ebira.cmd` to copy it into your per-user Programs folder and add a Start-menu shortcut, or run `Ebira.exe` directly. Node.js and a separately installed .NET runtime are not required.
+2. Install `Ebira-Android-development.apk` on your phone/tablet using Android's package installer. Enable installation for the local file manager only if needed. Public distribution signing is still pending.
+3. Connect Windows and Android to the same private Wi-Fi, or manually join an existing hotspot. In Windows Firewall, allow Ebira on the private network when prompted. The app prefers port 8443, falling back to an available port if occupied. Do not expose it through a router port-forward.
+4. Choose the Windows address belonging to that network. Candidate 2 prefers physical Wi-Fi/Ethernet over detected VPN/virtual adapters such as Cloudflare WARP; the dropdown still lets you choose another adapter. Copy its complete invitation to Android, paste it in Ebira, confirm it came from your Windows screen and tap Pair. If transferring the invitation manually is inconvenient, that is a known onboarding gap: QR/discovery is not implemented.
+5. Approve the named device in Windows. Android can now send additional files under that same approval. Use Start / resume for the existing session; end an old session before pairing again. In Windows, check approved recipients and choose Add files to send to them.
+6. Tap Start / resume transfers on Android to run its visible transfer service. Keep Windows awake. Select sequential or per-file round-robin in Windows when using more than one Android receiver.
+7. To resume after interruption, reopen both apps, start the Android service and use Resume on any paused jobs. An expired/ended session needs new pairing and new file offers. Keep selected sources unchanged and available until all recipients finish.
+
+Android photos/videos/audio publish to their respective Ebira media folders after checksum verification. Documents and other files appear under Download/Ebira. Windows receives under Downloads/Ebira by file type. Cancel transfers before cleaning incomplete files. Clearing history never deletes completed received files.
+
+The app records local resume metadata and encrypted credentials. There are no accounts, cloud transfer or analytics. See V1-BASELINE.md for persistence, retention and platform limitations, and DEVICE-VALIDATION.md for the remaining release tests.
+
+Updates are manual: stop Ebira and install the new Windows package into the same folder. Use an APK signed with the same owner-controlled key for Android upgrades; a development APK cannot be upgraded in place by a differently signed release. Back up completed files before uninstalling. User data is retained by the Windows uninstaller; Android uninstall removes private state but keeps published shared files.
+
+Support/defects: use this repository's GitHub Issues. Include device/OS/build, the transfer direction, file size and the exact visible error. Do not post pairing invitations, tokens, private keys, local state or personal file contents.
diff --git a/docs/POC.md b/docs/POC.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1da2e23
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/POC.md
@@ -0,0 +1,50 @@
+# Ebira — bare-bones PoC
+
+Legacy browser prototype retained for reference. This is not the native v1 candidate; run these commands from the repository root.
+
+This working prototype hosts a local encrypted transfer server on Windows. Android phones/tablets use their browser. It has no cloud service or external package dependencies.
+
+## Start
+
+Requirements: Windows with its built-in Windows PowerShell 5.1 and Node.js 20 or newer. PowerShell 7 (`pwsh`) is not required. The launcher also checks the standard Node.js installation folder if Node is missing from PATH.
+
+1. Double-click `Start-PoC.cmd` and keep the window open.
+2. Open the **Windows control** URL printed in that window on the Windows machine. Treat this URL as private: it grants host control.
+3. Connect your phone/tablet to the same Wi-Fi. Open the printed phone/tablet URL in Chrome.
+4. The PoC generates a self-signed HTTPS certificate, so browsers show a certificate warning. For personal testing on your trusted LAN, inspect the certificate and compare its SHA-256 fingerprint with the terminal before proceeding. The connection is encrypted, but certificate identity is not publicly trusted. Do not use this PoC on an untrusted network.
+5. Enter a device name and the pairing code from the Windows terminal. Approve that session in the Windows page.
+6. Select multiple files and press **Send selected files**. On Windows, choose the approved device in **Send to** first.
+
+Ebira prefers port 8443. If that port is occupied, it automatically selects an available port and prints the correct URLs. Always use the addresses from the current launcher window.
+
+Windows Firewall may need a private-network rule allowing Node.js or the TCP port printed in the launcher. Do not open this port on your router. If a guest Wi-Fi network blocks communication between devices, use a normal shared network or manually join an existing hotspot.
+
+## What works
+
+- HTTPS/TLS encryption, browser-to-Windows and Windows-to-browser transfers.
+- Approval once per device session, including subsequently added files.
+- Multiple files sent sequentially; individual file limit 10 GiB.
+- 4 MiB upload chunks, bounded server memory, progress and measured upload rate.
+- Stop/pause after the current chunk; resume by pressing Send while the same page/server remain open.
+- Received Windows files saved under `Downloads/Ebira/Pictures`, `Videos`, `Music`, `Documents`, or `Other`.
+- Collision-safe receive filenames. No overwrite of existing received files.
+- Receiver-side SHA-256 computation after upload; HTTPS authenticates transport records. This is not yet an independent sender/receiver checksum comparison.
+- Either side can end a session. Subsequent file requests require a new approved session.
+
+## PoC limitations
+
+- No native APK or standalone Windows installer yet. Phone/tablet browser downloads use the normal Downloads destination and require tapping Save per file. Android gallery albums and automatic type-based folders require the native app.
+- Windows stages outgoing files on disk before Android downloads them, using extra storage and an additional transfer step. This is not the final high-speed architecture.
+- Upload resumes only within the active page/server lifetime. Refreshing the sender page, restarting Windows, or restarting the server does not restore upload state.
+- No automatic nearby discovery, QR scan, Wi-Fi Direct management, or Maximum speed route selection yet.
+- No background-transfer guarantee: keep the browser foreground and screen awake.
+- TLS certificates need manual verification in this PoC; native certificate pinning and stronger pairing will replace this flow.
+- No malware scanning, disk-space preflight, download resume, persistent history, idle expiry, or automatic staging-file cleanup yet. Ending a session prevents new requests; a download already in progress can finish.
+- Outgoing and incomplete files remain in `data` until manually removed while the app is stopped. Windows completed incoming files remain in Downloads/Ebira. Private key material is also in `data`; do not share that directory.
+- The automatic checks exercise real HTTPS transfers with small binary files. Browser testing also passed a 16 MiB chunked upload and a reverse download using two separate browser sessions. The 10 GiB metadata limit is tested, but actual 5–10 GB transfers and device performance have not yet been validated on your hardware.
+
+## Tests
+
+Run `node --test test.js` from this folder after the first launch creates the local certificate.
+
+The tests cover session approval/revocation, two-way byte-identical transfer, chunk offsets/resume, session isolation, file-size limits, zero-byte files, and file categorization.
